## (C) Reachability summary

All six §3 per-claim outcomes: reachable. All seven §4 study-level classes: reachable.
UNREACHABLE verdicts: none. There is therefore no blocking clause to quote — no clause in the
V15 text blocks every path to any declared outcome or class. C0_REACHABILITY=PASS.

## The named suspicion — is `CENSUS_COMPLETE` reachable in a real corpus?

Answer: REACHABLE. The routing, end to end:

1. §4.1 defines the class over the arithmetic group of §3, and §3 defines that group as exactly
   `REPRO_EXACT` and `REPRO_FAILED`.
2. §3's precedence routes every claim into that group whenever its recipe is stated, every input
   the recipe names carries status `PRINTED` or `STANDARD` — chosen and fitted values included
   (§2 step 4: "using every value it directs you to use … chosen and fitted values included") —
   and the attempt completes within the 120-second cap on machinery the lane has. The four
   non-arithmetic outcomes each require a specific failure: no recipe stated
   (`REPRO_NO_DERIVATION_STATED`), a named source that is not enumerable or does not machine-match
   (`REPRO_BLOCKED`), an input traced to no source at all (`REPRO_INPUT_ABSENT`), or a
   timeout/machinery stall (`REPRO_NOT_EVALUABLE`).
3. §4's precedence files `CENSUS_COMPLETE` when no earlier class's condition holds: controls
   C0–C5b pass in every seat within two attempts (not `R3C2_NO_CLASS`, not
   `CENSUS_CONTROL_SPLIT`); the enumerations agree (not `CENSUS_DENOMINATOR_DISPUTED`); origin
   disputes affect at most 10% of included claims (not `CENSUS_ORIGIN_DISPUTED`); the audit
   reproduces every outcome and ledger and both seal receipts verify (not `CENSUS_AUDIT_FAILED`);
   and no included claim carries a non-arithmetic outcome (not `CENSUS_PARTIAL`).
4. Corpus Σ above is a concrete input satisfying every one of those conditions, so the class is
   exhibited and the text licenses it. No clause blocks the path; the class is not empty by
   construction.

The force the suspicion names is real but empirical, not textual. §4.2 makes any single included
claim carrying `REPRO_NO_DERIVATION_STATED`, `REPRO_BLOCKED`, `REPRO_INPUT_ABSENT` or
`REPRO_NOT_EVALUABLE` sufficient for `CENSUS_PARTIAL`, which "takes precedence over
`CENSUS_COMPLETE`" (and the §4 order lists `CENSUS_PARTIAL` immediately before
`CENSUS_COMPLETE`). So yes: one blocked, absent-input, no-derivation or stalled claim anywhere in
the corpus forces `CENSUS_PARTIAL`. What that establishes is that `CENSUS_COMPLETE`'s filing on
the actual pinned corpus is a property of that corpus, decidable only by running the census —
which is exactly the census's own question (§1: "does the paper's own number follow from the
paper's own recipe applied to the inputs it states?"). A class whose filing depends on the data
is not unreachable; a class is unreachable when the method contains no procedure that can ever
file it. This document's own record shows the difference: the retired `REPRO_AFTER_CHOICE` was
unreachable under the derivation-only wording because §2 then mandated an admissible-inputs-only
attempt with no second attempt specified (§10.2, §10.3) — a textual blockage, since removed by
the ruling. The same document's C0 rationale records the present asymmetry as the thing to check
early (§5, C0: "`CENSUS_COMPLETE` requires every included claim to carry an arithmetic-group
outcome, which a single blocked or absent input in the whole corpus is enough to prevent"), and
the V9 C0 record (§10.3) shows both prior blind seats independently found `CENSUS_COMPLETE`
reachable. This exhibition reaches the same conclusion on the V15 text, independently:
reachable — contingent on the corpus, blocked by no clause.

## The §1 dependence, stated

The brief notes §1's core definition is marked. As the V15 text stands: the HELD marker was
removed at V10 by the ruling adopting option (c) (§10.4); what remains marked in §1 is the
limitation, not a held clause — whether a numeral is "the paper's own result" remains a
judgement, moved from one reader to two who must agree, with `CENSUS_DENOMINATOR_DISPUTED` as
the declared stop. This exhibition takes §1 exactly as written and assumes no reading beyond it.
Consequences: every per-claim exhibit presupposes a §1 inclusion decision both seats could
reach on the stated passage; the `CENSUS_DENOMINATOR_DISPUTED` exhibit depends on that judgement
being exercisable differently by two readers — which the text itself licenses ("disagreement on
any candidate that survives two reconciliation attempts stops the study"). No row above carries
a held-clause contingency, because no held clause remains.

